{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310806,"nonce":"yo/HSnyUNMeMZSc4fGH7ebxurLM=","title":"Shuffle primitive SHIPPED (9d739dec) + a real LLVM lane-coercion bug fixed (cae1551f). The Q4 vector scale path itself: instructions -14.9% ...","message":"- **9d739dec `SIMD16i8.shuffle` / `make16`, `SIMD4i32.shuffleBytes` / `load`.**\n  One new `IrInstruction::VectorShuffle` carrying its result type, so one opcode\n  covers both a byte result and \"reinterpret shuffled bytes as i32 lanes\".\n  17 sites / 12 files; 4 fail SILENTLY if missed (`dest()`, `replace_dest()`,\n  `optimization.rs replace_uses`, `tiered_backend function_uses_simd`).\n  Index contract = INTERSECTION of pshufb/tbl1/i8x16.swizzle (0..15 selects,\n  bit 7 yields 0, 16..127 unspecified) so every backend emits ONE instruction.\n  Tests: `test_simd16i8_shuffle`, `test_q4k_scale_unpack` — pass on BOTH tiers.\n- **cae1551f REAL COMPILER BUG: `VectorSplat`/`VectorInsert` never coerced the\n  scalar to the vector's lane type**, so the lane width came from the VALUE.\n  Invisible until now because every SIMD type had matching scalars (f32→f32x4,\n  i32→i32x4); building i8x16 from Haxe Ints produced `\u003c16 x i32\u003e` and LLVM\n  verification failed. The tier then fell back to Cranelift **with only a\n  warning** — correct results, LLVM upgrade silently lost.\n\nVector scale path in `q4DotBody`: `q4Scales()` unpacks all 16 six-bit fields\ninto lanes with 2 shuffles + 5 logic ops; `bcast(S,j)` replaces\nshr/and/or→vmovd→vpbroadcastd with ONE shuffle; the mins reduction becomes\n4 × (`SIMD4i32.load` × shuffled mn pair).\n\n| tier | instructions | CYCLES |\n|---|---|---|\n| LLVM | 969.4G → 825.0G (**-14.9%**, 2/2) | 313.6G → 304.8G (**-2.8%**, 2/2) |\n| Cranelift | 2431G → 2257G (-7.2%) | 589.5G → 704.6G (**+19.5% WORSE**) |\n\nOutput BIT-IDENTICAL 3/3. Reverted; staged as `~/q4_vec.hx` on the NUC.\n\n**Why it was rejected:** a 2.8% gain on one tier does not justify a 19.5%\nregression on another. Cranelift lowers `swizzle` to `paddusb`+`pshufb` and\nschedules vectors poorly, and it is the baseline tier every program runs before\nthe LLVM upgrade (and the ONLY tier on wasm).\n\nI chose instruction count as the A/B metric because it was stable. It is the\nWRONG metric when a change alters the instruction MIX: IPC fell 3.09 → 2.71\n(-12%) because pshufb→vpmulld is a serial chain where the scalar GPR ops were\nindependent and dual-issued. A -14.9% instruction change was worth -2.8% time.\n**Use `cpu_core/cycles/` with `NUE_MATMUL_WORKERS=1`** — measured spread is\n0.02-0.08%, both stable AND the actual time proxy. Wall-clock ttft could not\nsee this at all (2/3 pairs read \"slower\" against 8-10% thermal drift).\n\nBefore adding a `SIMD8i16` type + widen/pairwise MIR ops across four backends,\nI bounded the prize: an arm that drops the sub-block scale ENTIRELY (wrong\nresults; `scLo*accLo + scHi*accHi` -\u003e `accLo + accHi`, so both the `vpmulld`\nand the broadcast vanish).\n\n    baseline  312.63G / 313.12G cycles\n    no-scale  297.94G / 296.72G cycles   -\u003e **-4.7% / -5.2%, 2/2**\n\n**Making the scale FREE is worth ~5% of cycles — that is the hard ceiling on\nStep 2.** Step 1 (the vector scale path) already captures -2.8% of it, so the\nADDITIONAL headroom is ~2% on LLVM, against a large new-primitive build and a\nlikely Cranelift regression (Step 1 was +19.5% there). **Not worth it.**\n\nThis probe is VALID where the earlier header-unpack one was not: values stay\nintegral (no denormal/FP-assist artefact), and instructions and cycles fell\nproportionally (IPC unchanged at 3.09), so it measures cost, not numerics.\n\n**The plan's \"Step 2 is the actual 2x\" was an inference from ggml's kernel\nDESIGN (0.383 instr/MAC vs our 1.46), not a measurement of our scale path's\nshare. Our scale path is only ~5% of cycles, so the 2x gap lives somewhere\nelse entirely — and is still unlocated.**\n\nggml's x86 q4_K **deliberately avoids VNNI**: `vpmaddubsw` (u8×s8→i16) then\n`vpmaddwd` (i16×i16→i32), because the second stage is a reduction the kernel\nneeds anyway and the per-sub-block scale rides it FOR FREE — no separate\n`vpmulld` at all. Saturation is safe (nibble ≤15 × |q8| ≤128, ×2 = 3840 \u003c\n32767). That needs a 16-bit lane type (`SIMD8i16` with mul/madd), which does\nNOT exist yet. The vector scale path is a PREREQUISITE for it, so re-evaluate\n`~/q4_vec.hx` together with Step 2 rather than on its own.\n\n`~/perf_clif.sh` had its `--preset development --preset-override-toml` stripped\nduring earlier cleanup, so a \"Cranelift\" A/B silently measured LLVM — the tell\nwas instruction counts matching the LLVM arm almost exactly (966G vs 969G).\n**Always confirm the tier moved: real Cranelift here is ~2.5x the instructions\nand ~2x the cycles.**\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `perf_q4_vector_scale_path_measured` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}